---
import FighterCanopyLight from "../../../components/studies/FighterCanopyLight.astro";
import FighterCockpitDepth from "../../../components/studies/FighterCockpitDepth.astro";
import FighterCockpits from "../../../components/studies/FighterCockpits.astro";
import FighterForms from "../../../components/studies/FighterForms.astro";
import FighterLowPoly from "../../../components/studies/FighterLowPoly.astro";
import FighterSlots from "../../../components/studies/FighterSlots.astro";
import FighterWingVariants from "../../../components/studies/FighterWingVariants.astro";
import ScoutSpacecraft from "../../../components/studies/ScoutSpacecraft.astro";
import ScoutWasp from "../../../components/studies/ScoutWasp.astro";
import ShipSilhouetteComparison from "../../../components/studies/ShipSilhouetteComparison.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "ship-silhouettes");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<aside aria-label="Study outcome">
		<p>
			<a href="#readiness">03 / Readiness</a>
			· <a href="#integration">04 / Integration plan</a>
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Exploration stopped · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			The usable outcome is
			<a href="#fighter-slots-tight">02.9: the agreed Fighter design</a>. Scout
			exploration did not produce a usable design. No Scout or Heavy Corvette
			design is accepted. Earlier examples remain as study evidence.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Implemented locally:</strong>
			the 02.9 Fighter now uses the shared ship designer and in-game rendering,
			preserving the game’s color theme and readable accents. Scout and Heavy
			Corvette selection is disabled until their designs are resolved. Inspect
			the result in <a href="/lab/builder">the builder Lab</a>
			and its test flight. Human fidelity and handling review remains pending.
		</p>
	</aside>
	<section aria-labelledby="purpose">
		<h2 id="purpose">01 / Three ships, recognizable from their arrangement</h2>
		<p>
			The builder and flight in 0.4.0-alpha.1 give us a foundation for three
			distinct ships: Scout, Fighter, and Heavy Corvette. This study asks how
			their arrangements can make each identity visible. Imagine choosing a ship
			in the builder, then recognizing it immediately when it becomes a small,
			rotating shape in flight. Its outline should carry that continuity.
		</p>
		<p>
			We will study overall proportions, where parts sit, the spaces between
			them, and engine placement and angles. A useful target is three shapes
			that remain distinguishable without labels or color; restrained color
			differences can reinforce those identities. Detailed part styling is
			outside this inquiry. Simple existing parts should let us judge the
			composition without surface detail making the decision for us.
		</p>
		<p>
			The central tension is between a clear composed silhouette and a ship
			whose editable parts and engine directions still make sense. The result
			should establish each ship’s recognizable arrangement in both the editor
			and the game. Engine angles contribute to appearance but also have flight
			consequences, so visual preference alone cannot settle their suitability.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Framing agreed · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			Recognition through arrangement guides the exploration; color supports
			identity and detailed part styling remains outside scope.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">02 / Finding the three outlines</h2>
		<h3 id="comparison">02.1 / Spine, shoulders, body</h3>
		<p>
			The smallest useful surface is one monochrome sheet: three current
			arrangements beside one proposed family. Can a narrow spine, swept
			shoulders, and a broad body give the ships distinct identities before
			color or part detail?
		</p>
		<p>
			These static sketches use the real chassis slot counts, hull/aft mount
			positions, and engine angles (32°, 26°, 20°). The proposal changes only
			the hull outline and side-engine positions. Filled rectangles stand for
			every slot, including empty ones; they are composition markers, not new
			part artwork.
		</p>
		<ShipSilhouetteComparison />
		<p>
			All ships face up at the same scale: enlarged above, then small at the
			production drawing scale of 0.65 below. The small view is a still size
			check, not a camera or flight simulation. No glow, exhaust, or color
			distinguishes the ships. Engine angles remain provisional; this sheet
			cannot establish flight handling or attachment feasibility.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>02.1 preserved · no design selected.</strong>
			This comparison helped reveal the problem, without choosing a ship family.
			Its sketches remain unchanged. The next investigation deliberately narrows
			to the default Fighter; Scout and Corvette identities remain unresolved.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fighter-forms">02.2 / Fighter: sleek surfaces, sharp intent</h3>
		<p>
			The first sheet missed the intended aesthetic. The intended direction is
			sleek turn-of-the-millennium vector art: luminous surfaces, clean forms,
			controlled glow. Arrow and perhaps Clipped wings were close; Barbed arrow
			might offer something. None was selected. The finding is a missing shared
			visual direction, not simply a shortage of silhouette options.
		</p>
		<p>
			This revised sheet explores three restrained triangular forms in that
			surface language. Aggression comes from the long nose and swept
			proportions. Each uses the same cyan palette, filled facets, dark inset,
			and soft bloom; there are no outline strokes. These are provisional
			composition treatments, not detailed part designs.
		</p>
		<FighterForms />
		<p>
			Slots remain visible using the current Fighter's mount positions and 26°
			engine angles. The inset and glow are study-only treatments. The flat
			shape beside each rendering keeps the silhouette inspectable without the
			lighting. All three share drawing scales; the turned views are static, not
			flight or camera evidence. Mount integration and the other ship types
			remain open.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Does this feel closer to the sleek vector-art Fighter—and which surface
				or edge treatment carries that feeling?</strong
			>
			We should establish that visual language before adding more shapes.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/lab/studies/archive/fighter-forms-first-pass"
				>Preserved first 02.2 sheet and the design finding</a
			>
			· superseded, no design selected. 02.1 remains unchanged.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fighter-low-poly">02.3 / Six Fighters in low-poly form</h3>
		<p>
			The revised 02.2 forms felt promising; the next step was more shape
			choices with a low-poly treatment. That sheet is preserved as a useful
			comparison, with no individual ship selected. This round keeps the angry,
			pointed Fighter brief and explores six distinct proportions and wing
			arrangements.
		</p>
		<p>
			Solid, flat-shaded polygon faces suggest a raised spine and sloped wings.
			A consistent light side and dark side make the surface volumes readable
			without outline strokes, gradients, or bloom. The same current Fighter
			slots and engine angles remain visible as dark markers. They are
			placeholders for placement, not part artwork.
		</p>
		<FighterLowPoly />
		<p>
			Each card shows the form large, then turned and small, plus a flat
			silhouette. The palette and drawing scales are shared across all six.
			These are 2D low-poly style sketches, not 3D models or tested flight
			configurations; mount support and final part integration remain
			unresolved.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Which of these six has the strongest Fighter character—and does the
				faceted volume feel like the right direction?</strong
			>
			Shape and surface treatment can be judged separately.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fighter-wings">
			02.4 / Lance and Cutlass: small wings, clean angles
		</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Agreed direction:</strong>
			02.3's low-poly graphic style was agreed as the right direction. Lance (A)
			and Cutlass (C) are the promising forms to develop. Trident (E) and Dart
			(F) are outside the intended direction. The earlier sheet remains intact;
			no final Fighter silhouette is selected.
		</p>
		<p>
			These six variations stay between the slim Lance and clipped-shoulder
			Cutlass. Small swept winglets and single angle changes add character while
			keeping one dominant forward point. The faceted palette, central ridge,
			scale, and lighting treatment are carried forward. Slot placement is
			deferred; its markers are omitted here so the hull and wing relationships
			can be read clearly.
		</p>
		<FighterWingVariants />
		<p>
			A–C develop the Lance end of the range; D–F develop the Cutlass. Each has
			a large view, a smaller turned view, and a flat silhouette. These static
			surface sketches do not establish mount support or flight handling.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Which balance of slender nose and small swept wings gives the Fighter
				the right character?</strong
			>
			Useful details can be combined without changing the agreed graphic style.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fighter-cockpits">02.5 / A flight deck for the Lance</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Chosen Fighter hull:</strong>
			02.4 A / Lance · clipped tips. This form was selected along with the
			low-poly style. This round holds its outline, facets, palette, and ridge
			unchanged while finding a visible place for the flight deck.
		</p>
		<p>
			Six controlled variations: three positions along the centerline, each
			shown inset into the ridge and on a small raised canopy base. The dark
			glazing has the same size and shape in all six. Compare A with D, B with
			E, and C with F to separate placement from the raised treatment.
		</p>
		<FighterCockpits />
		<p>
			The large and turned views include the flight deck; the flat silhouette
			confirms the unchanged hull. The canopy suggests volume through flat
			shading in this 2D sketch. Slot placement, visibility in motion, and
			production integration remain outside this round.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Where does the flight deck feel at home: forward, central, or
				rearward—and should it sit within the ridge or rise slightly above
				it?</strong
			>
		</p>

		<h3 id="fighter-canopy-light">02.6 / Cockpit as a crisp accent</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>All soft canopy treatments rejected.</strong>
			The hull is approaching the intended game aesthetic, but the cockpit must
			share its crisp colors and heavy accents. The forward position and
			selected low-poly Lance remain settled. The previous gradients and blurred
			glow did not meet that direction.
		</p>
		<p>
			Six new treatments use the game's existing acid-lime and void colors.
			Opaque filled shapes, sharp edges, and strong light–dark separation make
			the cockpit a deliberate accent. The choices vary the pane shape and
			distribution of that accent: solid, split, brow, or side panels. No
			gradients, blur, transparency, or glass reflections are used on the
			cockpit.
		</p>
		<FighterCanopyLight />
		<p>
			The same hull and forward anchor appear in every card. Below each large
			ship are a small turned ship and a canopy detail on a simplified hull
			patch. The dark area is a flat graphic surround, not the rejected faceted
			canopy. These are appearance studies; slot layout and flight readability
			remain untested.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Which treatment gives the forward cockpit a clear, deliberate presence
				within the game's graphic language?</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/lab/studies/archive/fighter-canopy-soft"
				>Rejected soft canopy sheet and feedback</a
			>
			· preserved separately, no option accepted.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fighter-cockpit-depth">02.7 / A place inside the ship</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>02.6's crisp-accent remake was also rejected.</strong>
			The cockpit's purpose is a small integrated focal point that gives the
			ship character, makes it feel finished and flyable, and can carry neon
			identity color. The useful evidence remains 02.5 A's dark forward spot at
			small scale. Its detailed canopy design was not accepted.
		</p>
		<p>
			This comparison returns to that exact reference. B tests color within a
			dark recess; C tests color across the canopy surface. Both use the same
			saturated cyan. The selected hull, forward position, cockpit footprint,
			and viewing scales stay fixed. No frames, new silhouettes, blur, or
			additional ship details are introduced.
		</p>
		<FighterCockpitDepth />
		<p>
			Each card contains a large ship, a small turned ship below left, and a
			magnified cockpit on a simplified hull patch below right. A reproduces
			02.5 A. B and C are hypotheses about visual depth and character, not
			selected designs. Different identity hues and visibility during flight
			remain untested.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Does B or C retain the sense of a place inside the ship while adding
				the right focal color—or does the reference still work better?</strong
			>
			First compare the small ships, then check whether the close-up supports
			that impression.
		</p>

		<h3 id="fighter-slots">02.8 / Slots on the Lance</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Cockpit settled for this study:</strong>
			the forward reference footprint becomes one solid black shape. B was
			closest to the classic space-game references, but still felt smudgy and
			broken. Neon cockpit exploration stops here; identity color remains
			unresolved.
		</p>
		<p>
			The selected clipped-tip Lance now carries six translucent mounting areas.
			The two side engines sit rearward, parallel to the long hull edges (about
			17° from the centerline). The two face slots form a column immediately
			behind the cockpit; the aft pair sit side by side across the stern.
		</p>
		<FighterSlots />
		<p>
			This sketch uses the Fighter's existing slot inventory with proposed
			positions and sizes. Engine alignment is a starting proposal; slow turning
			is an intended feel, not something this static view verifies. Parts and
			flight behavior are unchanged.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Does this placement match the fighter you have in mind, especially the
				stacked face slots and rearward engine pair?</strong
			>
		</p>
		<h3 id="fighter-slots-tight">02.9 / Larger mounts, rotated engines</h3>
		<p>
			02.8 established a promising composition. This refinement enlarges the
			mounting areas, narrows the gaps between them, and brings the aft pair
			inward. The original layout stays above for comparison.
		</p>
		<FighterSlots compact />
		<p>
			<strong>Fighter design agreed · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			This exact composition is agreed as the Fighter design: low-poly Lance
			with clipped tips, black forward cockpit, large stacked face slots,
			central aft pair, and rotated side-engine slots sitting forward and partly
			outside the chassis. Preserve this geometry and placement. Production
			integration and flight handling remain untested.
		</p>
		<p>
			The following Scout exploration was stopped without a usable result. Its
			identity should be distinct from the Fighter's angry triangle; no Scout
			silhouette has been selected.
		</p>
		<h3 id="scout-forms">
			02.10 / Scout: a compact Wasp, shaped by its mounts
		</h3>
		<p>
			The roadster and fast-boat sheets were rejected for excessive complexity
			and lack of character. The new direction is a compact, cockpit-first Wasp:
			nimble and sporty, with the component arrangement giving the ship its
			shape.
		</p>
		<p>
			One proposal: two side engines high on the shoulders, ahead of the single
			hull slot, with one aft engine low at the rear. The hull is 80% of the
			Fighter's length. A broad forward cabin tapers into a simple rear body;
			translucent slots keep the placement visible without designing the parts.
		</p>
		<ScoutWasp />
		<p>
			The existing Scout slot inventory is retained. Side-engine angles start at
			the current Scout's 32° and are provisional. This sketch examines
			arrangement and character; it does not establish flight handling. The
			small views compare hull length at the same scale.
		</p>
		<p>
			The arrangement is the strongest Scout direction so far. The hexagonal
			window and tapered hull read as a coffin; the silhouette is not selected.
		</p>
		<h3 id="scout-wasp-forms">02.11 / Six compact spacecraft</h3>
		<p>
			The previous six outlines were rejected in full. This remake starts with
			recognizable spacecraft volumes: a forward cabin, two shoulder engine
			housings, a short central fuselage, and one aft drive. Classic small
			science-fiction ships guide the character; insect shapes no longer guide
			the outline.
		</p>
		<p>
			Six compositions vary cabin width, engine separation, sweep, and body
			proportions. The side drives stay ahead of the hull bay. A rounded dark
			canopy sits within each faceted cabin; a few broad surfaces describe the
			body and engine housings. No slot overlays.
		</p>
		<ScoutSpacecraft />
		<p>
			All six share the Fighter's palette and a length of 80% of its hull. Small
			turned views test the whole composition. Housings are simple volume
			studies, not final part artwork; mount fit, engine angles, and flight
			handling remain provisional.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Exploration stopped. No design from 02.11 is selected.</strong>
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Readiness: the Fighter can be integrated</h2>
		<p>
			02.9 is the accepted visual result. Its clipped-tip Lance, black forward
			cockpit, stacked hull slots, central aft pair, and rotated side engines
			can be implemented with the existing procedural drawing system. The
			three-ship exploration did not reach its original goal: Scout and Heavy
			Corvette remain unresolved. Integration is limited to the Fighter.
		</p>
		<h3>What already fits</h3>
		<p>
			The Fighter already has the required two mirrored side engines, two
			independent hull slots, and two independent aft slots. Its saved loadout
			stores part choices by slot, not coordinates. Moving those slots therefore
			does not require deleting saves or changing their identifiers. Both the
			builder and custom flight call the same drawBuild renderer. The Lab
			builder uses the same components as the game, so it can demonstrate the
			real result.
		</p>
		<h3>What must change together</h3>
		<p>
			The accepted geometry currently exists only in the study. Production
			buildMounts still places the hull slots side by side and uses smaller
			footprints. A shared Fighter definition must supply the hull, facets,
			cockpit, slot positions, orientations, and sizes to rendering, selection
			targets, and exhaust origins. Existing fittings can be adapted to the new
			mounting areas; detailed part redesign remains outside this integration.
		</p>
		<p>
			The consequential mismatch is the side-engine angle: approximately 66.8°
			in 02.9, versus 26° in current Fighter calculations. Flight and estimates
			currently use chassis angle and arm separately from drawing geometry.
			Changing only the picture would make thrust disagree with the visible
			engines. The integration must use consistent mount directions and account
			for their rearward offset when deriving the turning lever arm. The current
			direct turning model can remain, but its response must be reviewed in
			flight. The accepted picture does not establish acceptable handling.
		</p>
		<h3>Color, selection, and scale</h3>
		<p>
			Keep the accepted faceted light-and-dark structure and black cockpit. Use
			the game's existing neon accents for fittings, selection, and focus; check
			contrast against the hull and space background. Custom rendering currently
			hard-codes its colors, while stock ships accept a profile tint. There is
			no implemented team system to preserve. This work concerns the game's
			visual theme; adding teams is outside scope.
		</p>
		<p>
			The accepted hull is longer than the current builder Fighter. Builder
			framing, slot hit targets, small flight readability, and collision-size
			consistency need checks. The tightly paired aft slots especially need
			distinct selection targets. Preserve keyboard operation and flight touch
			controls. No new rendering dependency is needed.
		</p>
		<h3>Readiness conclusion</h3>
		<p>
			The existing architecture supports a bounded Fighter integration. The
			remaining risks are geometry consistency, fitting readability, and flight
			feel—not a need for another silhouette round. Code inspection supports
			feasibility; no integrated rendering or new handling has been verified
			yet.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">04 / Integration plan: one finished Fighter</h2>
		<p>
			Deliver the 02.9 Fighter in the ship designer and custom flight.
			Temporarily disable Scout and Heavy Corvette selection in the designer,
			with a short explanation that their designs are pending. Preserve their
			saved builds and underlying definitions. Existing stock flight and scored
			profiles remain outside this builder-focused change.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Promote the agreed geometry.</strong>
				Extract the 02.9 hull, facet palette, cockpit, and mount layout into a
				shared procedural Fighter definition. Keep the study reference intact.
				Adapt buildMounts and drawBuild to use it, including the larger
				translucent empty slots and fittings seated at the same positions.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Connect placement to use.</strong>
				Update ShipBuilder selection targets and preview framing from the shared
				layout. Keep every slot independently selectable, including the aft
				pair. Disable the two unresolved chassis without changing the
				three-build storage contract; open the Fighter by default and preserve
				saved part choices.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Align flight and exhaust.</strong>
				Update build-flight and buildStats to consume consistent engine
				directions and turning geometry. Have buildPlumeEmitters originate at
				the actual nozzle positions. Retain energy budgeting, speed limits,
				shared plume effects, and the existing gravity and friction constants.
				Review scale and collision alignment rather than silently accepting a
				larger visual footprint.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Apply the game theme.</strong>
				Keep the approved silhouette, facets, and black cockpit while making
				installed parts, empty mounts, selection, and focus readable with shared
				game accents. Review the same fitted ship in the designer and flight
				before choosing any further palette adjustment. Do not restart cockpit
				or hull exploration.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Verify and document.</strong>
				Demonstrate the production result in /lab/builder and its test flight.
				Update the ship-builder specification and relevant design documentation
				with the Fighter-only availability, shared geometry, saved-build
				compatibility, and remaining tuning limits. Record implementation
				separately from this plan.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<h3>Verification and review</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Automated checks: slot compatibility, mirrored fitting, persistence,
				preservation of disabled-chassis saves, corruption recovery, and
				disabled selection. Replace the browser test that currently requires all
				three chassis to be selectable with the new availability contract.
			</li>
			<li>
				Geometry and simulation checks: nozzle-to-plume alignment, mirrored
				thrust cancellation, left/right steering, aft activation, displayed
				estimates matching flight calculations, energy behavior, and overspeed
				protection. Run repository verification and report failures or
				unavailable checks explicitly.
			</li>
			<li>
				Visual inspection: compare against 02.9 with empty slots and a fitted
				build; inspect each slot's selected state, keyboard focus, the tight aft
				pair, and the small moving ship with exhaust. Inspect intended visual
				changes before updating any screenshot baseline.
			</li>
			<li>
				Human review: confirm fidelity to 02.9, usable game colors, assembly
				readability, and acceptable single-engine and paired-engine handling.
				Passing automated checks cannot establish those qualities.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<strong>Completion means:</strong>
			the same agreed Fighter is editable and flyable with aligned parts and
			exhaust; only the Fighter is available in the designer; existing saves are
			retained; checks and remaining human review findings are reported. No
			Scout or Heavy Corvette design is implied.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Implementation · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			The Fighter is implemented in the shared builder and custom flight. The
			plan and readiness assessment above record the pre-integration findings.
			Saved builds are retained. Side thrust and displayed estimates use the
			66.8° mounts and rearward offset; this produces counterclockwise
			left-engine turning, clockwise right-engine turning, and about 18.5°/s
			with default engines. Exhaust starts at each actual nozzle. The 0.65
			drawing scale is retained; a conservative 31.2-unit circular collision
			envelope includes the longer nose. Its lateral overestimate and flight
			feel remain human-review questions.
			<a href="/lab/builder">Review the editable, flyable Fighter</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Flight-review correction · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			The owner found the side engines too widely rotated and chose to retain
			the original production angle of 26°. The side pair then moved inward from
			±18 to ±15 units at the owner’s request. Fore–aft positions and sizes are
			unchanged. Fittings, exhaust, and flight calculations share this angle.
			This supersedes the 02.9 engine orientation; that sketch remains unchanged
			as earlier evidence. Default fission turning is now about 96.6°/s after a
			2.5× angular-response adjustment following feedback that it still turned
			too slowly, with clockwise left-engine turning and counterclockwise
			right-engine turning. Handling remains open for further flight review.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
